WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.219
Safe logging continues after code writes the event.

2
00:00:03.219 --> 00:00:09.656
Logs are collected, transported, indexed, searched, retained, exported, and sometimes copied into tickets or incident records.

3
00:00:09.656 --> 00:00:12.472
Governance has to cover the whole lifecycle.

4
00:00:12.472 --> 00:00:16.898
Access control is essential because logs often aggregate sensitive operational context.

5
00:00:16.898 --> 00:00:24.542
Even if individual events avoid obvious secrets, combined logs can reveal identifiers, timing, system behavior, environment names, and business

6
00:00:24.542 --> 00:00:27.359
activity that should not be broadly visible.

7
00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:30.980
Retention rules should be defined before storage grows indefinitely.

8
00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.394
Some logs support short-term debugging.

9
00:00:33.394 --> 00:00:35.808
Others support audit or incident response.

10
00:00:35.808 --> 00:00:43.050
Each category should have a retention period, deletion policy, and owner who understands the operational and privacy tradeoffs.

11
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.269
Redaction and classification policies should be reviewed regularly.

12
00:00:46.269 --> 00:00:51.499
Field names, data sources, third-party libraries, and diagnostic practices change over time.

13
00:00:51.499 --> 00:00:57.534
A field that was safe in one version may become sensitive after a feature change.

14
00:00:57.534 --> 00:00:59.143
Separate environments and audiences.

15
00:00:59.143 --> 00:01:05.983
Development logs, test logs, production logs, and regulated operational records may require different access, retention, and masking.

16
00:01:05.983 --> 00:01:10.409
Copying production-style logs into less controlled places should be governed.

17
00:01:10.409 --> 00:01:14.432
Logging changes should be reviewed like other security-sensitive code.

18
00:01:14.432 --> 00:01:21.272
Adding a new event, changing a field, increasing verbosity, or expanding retention can change the risk profile.

19
00:01:21.272 --> 00:01:26.100
Governance keeps logging useful without allowing it to become unmanaged data collection.

20
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.916
Operational governance should also include periodic sampling.

21
00:01:28.916 --> 00:01:36.561
Reviewing real events helps teams find accidental sensitive fields, noisy messages, missing correlation, and retention categories that no longer

22
00:01:36.561 --> 00:01:38.975
match how the system is used.

23
00:01:38.975 --> 00:01:40.584
Ownership should be explicit.

24
00:01:40.584 --> 00:01:49.033
Someone should be accountable for the logging policy, the field taxonomy, access approvals, retention exceptions, and the process for updating events

25
00:01:49.033 --> 00:01:50.240
when requirements change.
